Assign Switch Ports to VLANs:
-----------------------------
We will tell the switch:
    fa0/1 belongs to FINANCE (VLAN 10)
    fa0/2 belongs to HR (VLAN 20)

    Why: because the PCs are physically connected to these ports:
    -------------------------------------------------------------
        PC0 -------- Fa0/1 -------- Switch
                          VLAN 10

        PC1 -------- Fa0/2 -------- Switch
                                  VLAN 20

    * without assigning the ports, the switch doesn't know which VLAN the PCs belong to.

    Configure fa0/1:
    ----------------
    enable
    configure terminal
    interface fastEthernet0/1
    switchport mode access
    switchport access vlan 10  (your telling to the switch whatever device is connected to fa0/1 belongs to VLAN 10)
    no shutdown
    exit

    configure terminal
    interface fastEthernet0/2
    switchport mode access
    switchport access vlan 20
    no shutdown
    exit

    verify: show vlan brief
    ------------------------
    VLAN 10   Finance   Fa0/1
    VLAN 20   HR        Fa0/2


    Why: switchport mode access:
    ----------------------------
    A switch port can work in two modes:

    1) Access Port: Carries traffic only one VLAN
        PC -------- Switch
           Access Port

        * since a PC belongs to only one VLAN, we use Access Mode

    2) Trunk Port: Carries traffic multiple VLANs
        Switch ===== Router
                Trunk


    What did we just to:
    --------------------
    We told the switch:
        PC0 -> VLAN 10
        PC1 -> VLAN 20

    - At this point, the PCs are isolated from each other because they are in different VLANs.

    Think:
    ------
    1) If PC0 (VLAN 10) tries to ping PC1 (VLAN 20) right now, will it work? Why or why not:
      - No, PC0 cannot ping PC1 because they belong to different VLANs,
      - A VLAN creates a separate Layer 2 broadcast domain
      - Even through both PCs are connected to teh same physical switch,
        the switch treats VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 as separate logical switches
      - since a layer 2 switch cannot route traffic between different VLANs,
      - communication is not possible until Layer 3 device (Router or Layer 3 Switch)
        performs inter-VLAN routing.


    Now let's understand WHY:
    -------------------------
     +----------------------+
     |      Switch          |
     |                      |
     |  VLAN 10   VLAN 20   |
     |                      |
     +----------------------+
        |             |
      PC0           PC1

     suppose PC0 sends a frame:
     will the switch send it to PC1 ?, NO

     why: because the switch first checks: which VLAN did this frame come from
     VLAN 10: then it asks, which ports belong to VLAN 10, only fa0/1
     it never even looks at VLAN 20
     so, the frame is never forwarded to PC1
     this is why VLANs provided isolation

How can we make them communicate:
---------------------------------
PC0 is in 192.168.10.0/24
PC1 is in 192.168.20.0/24
These are two different IP networks

Who is responsible for moving packets between different networks:
-----------------------------------------------------------------
A switch
A router
A PC

>> Router or L3 Switch


Let's understand with an example:
---------------------------------
PC0
IP : 192.168.10.10
Mask : 255.255.255.0
Gateway : 192.168.10.1

↓

Fa0/1

↓

Switch

↓

Fa0/2

↓

PC2
IP : 192.168.10.20
Mask : 255.255.255.0
Gateway : 192.168.10.1

Both PCs are:
-------------
Same VLAN (VLAN 10)
Same subnet (192.168.10.0/24)

Can they communicate: Yes
-------------------------
Because they are in the same network and same VLAN. The switch forwards the Ethernet frames directly after learning the destination MAC address.



Now let's change only one thing:
--------------------------------
PC0
192.168.10.10
VLAN 10

↓

Switch

↓

PC1
192.168.20.20
VLAN 20


Can they communicate: No
------------------------
Different VLANs
Different IP networks

The Layer 2 switch says: "I know how to switch frames inside one VLAN, but I don't know how to move packets between different IP networks
So it needs help from a Router or a Layer 3 Switch.


Suppose:
--------
PC0
IP : 192.168.10.10
Gateway : 192.168.10.1

PC1
IP : 192.168.20.20
Gateway : 192.168.20.1

PC0 wants to send data to 192.168.20.20:
----------------------------------------
A) Send the packet directly to PC1?
B) Send the packet to its default gateway (192.168.10.1)?

@B) Send the packet to its default gateway (192.168.10.1).

Why: PC0 first checks:
-----------------------
"Is 192.168.20.20 in my network?"

It compares:
My network: 192.168.10.0/24
Destination: 192.168.20.20

They are different:
This destination is outside my local network. I cannot send directly. I'll send it to my default gateway
The default gateway (the router or Layer 3 switch) then decides how to reach 192.168.20.20

We have only one cable between the switch and the router:
---------------------------------------------------------
           VLAN 10
PC0 --------\
             \
              Switch ----------- Router
             /
PC1 --------/
           VLAN 20

Question: That one cable has to carry traffic for: VLAN 10, VLAN 20

How will the router know:
-------------------------
"This packet belongs to VLAN 10."
"This packet belongs to VLAN 20."

That's exactly why we need a trunk port and 802.1Q VLAN tagging.